<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Georg Truchsess von Waldburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1488">1488</a>–<a href="/years/1531">1531</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Bauernjörg, Georg III, Truchsess von Waldburg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Georg III, Truchsess von Waldburg, known as Bauernjörg, was a Swabian noble who commanded the army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-league">Swabian League</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-peasants-war">German Peasants' War</a> of 1525. His victories at Leipheim, Böblingen and Königshofen crushed the peasant armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a> and earned him his nickname. He later served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> as governor of Württemberg.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/georg-truchsess-von-waldburg.jpg" alt="Georg Truchsess von Waldburg" width="320" height="461" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christoph Amberger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bauernjoerg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Georg III Truchsess von Waldburg-Zeil was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-waldsee">Waldsee</a> on 25 January 1488 into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-waldburg">House of Waldburg</a>, a Swabian noble family. Through him the house received in 1525 the hereditary title of Truchsess, the steward or seneschal of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, and the right to bear it in the family name. Together with his cousin Wilhelm he received the hereditary title of Imperial Steward from Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> on 27 July 1526.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1508 Georg served Duke Ulrich von Württemberg and helped him crush the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poor-conrad">Poor Conrad</a> rebellion. In 1516 he fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> beside Emperor Maximilian I in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and its allies. He then entered the service of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-league">Swabian League</a> and drove his former master Ulrich out of Württemberg, and in 1525 he succeeded his cousin Wilhelm as governor of the duchy.</p><p>When the peasants rose in 1525, most imperial troops were fighting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and Georg could at first recruit only 4,000 unreliable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landsknecht">Landsknechts</a>, too few to do more than negotiate. After the imperial victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pavia">Battle of Pavia</a>, veterans returning to southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> joined his army. Under his command one peasant army after another was defeated, and the fugitives were pursued into their villages to be tortured and executed. Sources speak of between 70,000 and 130,000 peasants killed.</p><p>After the rising was crushed, Georg ruled large parts of the territories he had conquered and collected ransoms from the people who remained. His harsh and pitiless conduct towards the rebels gave him the name by which he became known, Bauernjörg.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Georg died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-waldsee">Bad Waldsee</a> on 29 May 1531, six years after the Peasants' War had made his name. He appears as a character in two historical novels: Lichtenstein by Wilhelm Hauff, and The Adventurer, also published as Michael the Finn, by Mika Waltari.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Georg, Truchsess von Waldburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Georg III, Truchsess of Waldburg</b>, commanded the army of the Swabian League that crushed the peasant revolt in 1525, earning the nickname 'Bauernjörg'.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-waldburg">County of Waldburg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-waldsee">Bad Waldsee</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-waldsee">Bad Waldsee</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.92,9.75&amp;year=1515" aria-label="Bad Waldsee on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Georg Truchsess von Waldburg"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5556 5984h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5556 5984h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.92,9.75&amp;year=1515">What was here in 1515? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
